WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.020
- I wanna say this too though.

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:04.110
So Cedric, you played Dr.
King's closest friend,

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.910
Ralph Abernathy.
- Yeah.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.380
- In a Spike Lee movie.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.750
So did you learn anything about Dr. King

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.510
that surprised you that maybe wasn't ever,

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:13.770
isn't showcased on the regular?

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:15.330
- Well, in this movie "Son of South"

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.400
I actually had studied the dude,

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.660
I've been cast for Ralph
Abernathy in "Selma" too.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.458
And so, but,

00:00:23.458 --> 00:00:25.230
what I found out the most is their,

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:26.130
their sense of humor.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.800
What I really loved is
between the two of them

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.360
they were really friends

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:33.240
and they had this buddy
energy that I think

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.850
that you miss when someone is such a

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:37.380
a great history, you know,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.380
historic figure is that you
always see them as serious.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:41.760
And what I really loved is

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.560
that they were friends and buddies

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.110
and had this shorthand with
each other and laughed a lot.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:49.140
And so therefore, you know, it was that

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.210
that understanding that,
how do you do a work

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:54.960
like that, when every
day seems so serious?

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.450
You have to have some degree of like

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.057
where life is comfortable and you,

00:01:00.057 --> 00:01:01.350
and that was his partner.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.397
And I like that. I loved the,

00:01:03.397 --> 00:01:05.220
(audience clapping)
The idea that...

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:06.120
- Well, it's interesting to me,

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.710
- they had each other for that.

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:09.000
- It's interesting to
me too, though, I find

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.850
like the most deep people I
know are usually comedians,

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.007
even if they're, it's not their job.

00:01:13.007 --> 00:01:15.870
They're the people that
make you laugh so hard.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.000
And they have such a deep understanding,

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.500
a deep intelligence for a lot of things.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:20.971
And it is, they are
the more serious people

00:01:20.971 --> 00:01:22.431
in my life as well.

00:01:22.431 --> 00:01:25.170
- But I think you have to
find that humor to have that,

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:28.200
If balance existed, but to
have that kind of balance, too,

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.690
because you wanna address
things and you wanna be a

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.880
part of things but at the same
time it can be overwhelming

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.250
and it feels so big that you
can't conquer it, you know?

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.543
So you need humor.

